From f8fdf2bf9a0303c72599c4ae68dc3b198dccdc5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:58:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 094/240] Bugfix: split column blocks according to the computed
 ordering in `hgraphOrderBl()` and `hmeshOrderBl()` [report R. Perry]

The blocking method did not do what its documentation says, that is,
traverse the column block tree produced by its strategy and split the
leaves "without changing the ordering that has been computed":

 - when the strategy produced a tree, the recursion on the sub-blocks
   called `hgraphOrderBl()` itself, which ran the strategy again on
   the whole graph for every sub-block, and did not terminate for
   tree-producing strategies such as "b{strat=n{...}}";
 - for vertex-weighted graphs, loads were summed in local vertex
   order (since a90b1cb, through the inverse permutation before, which
   indexed the local load array with original vertex indices for
   induced subgraphs), while block boundaries apply to ordering
   positions; blocks were therefore unbalanced as soon as the ordering
   was not the identity (e.g., "b{cmin=1200,strat=g}": block weights
   from 24 to 16930 instead of 954 to 1577);
 - trailing zero-weight columns were left out of all blocks, yielding
   an inconsistent column block tree.

Run the strategy once, compute the column loads in ordering order
(directly through the inverse permutation for original graphs, by
matching original vertex indices for induced subgraphs), and split
the leaves of the tree recursively in a separate routine, keeping
track of the ordering number of each sub-block. The last block of a
weighted split receives any remaining zero-weight columns. Blocks
that cannot be split into at least two sub-blocks are kept as leaves.

Refs: GRA-22, PS2-G15-2, PS2-G15-3
